From 9c55f200624575939c19e598304b789f3fc2403c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:06:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(sink): run rejection beside SimpleCPUOffloadConnector
 with kv_both

Upstream base: the patch applies to local-inference-lab/vllm at
19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354 after the 702 patches of
spark-ds41f-1120 (candidate-62, 0001 to 0829) and patches 9030 to 9045
of this series.

Rationale: sink rejection is on by default for DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, and
the sink check of the V2 runner refused every KV connector while
rejection was on, so a start with the disk tier failed at model load on
every rank with "The sink detector and rejection (vllm.v1.sample.sink)
do not support KV transfer." No text gives a reason for that entry; the
message of patch 0221 calls the list "the features it does not handle".
A reading of the scheduler side of the connector found no path on which
the connector changes a request that holds a provisional token. Loads
are asynchronous only, and they reach requests in WAITING_FOR_REMOTE_KVS,
which hold no draft zero. A failed or overrun load comes back as
failed_recving for those requests only, and the connector reports no
invalid blocks, so the path that cuts the computed tokens of a running
request without clearing draft zero never runs. A hit leaves at least
the last prompt token to compute, and 128 tokens with the replay suffix
of the V4.1 layout. Stores copy hashed blocks only, which hold committed
tokens, and the eager store also stops at the computed tokens. The
connector does not ask for dropped stale outputs at a preemption. This
patch therefore adds a pure function, kv_transfer_refusal in
vllm/v1/sample/sink.py, that allows SimpleCPUOffloadConnector with
kv_role kv_both when it is set as kv_connector and taken from the vLLM
registry. Every other connector or role, a MultiConnector, and a
kv_connector_module_path are still refused, and the refusal names the
connector and the role, or a config with no kv_connector set, together
with the one allowed configuration. The detector without rejection runs
beside any connector, as before, and no other product code changes. The
reading may have missed a case for which the entry was written.

Quality implications: where the reading holds, outputs do not change. A
case that the reading missed would most likely show as an assertion in
the worker or the scheduler, because the scheduler checks that draft
zero leads every step of a decoding request. Every other entry of the
refusal list stays in force.

Tests: the patch adds 58 tests on the CPU path. 48 of them run sink
rejection through the core scheduler, in sync and async mode, with no
drafter and with 2 drafts, beside the scheduler side of the connector
with the disk backend, the policy "recompute", and one rank. A scripted
worker keeps the KV cache as (position, token) slots, checks the slots
that each step reads, checks each store slot by slot against the
committed tokens of the block, and copies tier slots back on each load.
After each output, the computed tokens past the committed ones and the
output placeholders must stay within the tokens in flight. At the end of
each run, the key of every hashed block on the GPU and on the tier is
recomputed from the block's slots, and the block hashes of every
request from its committed tokens. The cases are a tier hit followed by
a draft-zero rejection; a failed load; an overrun load and its cold
retry; a preemption while draft zero is held and a resume with a tier
hit, with no replay suffix or a suffix of 16 tokens, in one step or in
chunks of 16 tokens; an abort in WAITING_FOR_REMOTE_KVS beside a request
that holds a draft zero; and a rejection at the last slot of a block in
eager and lazy mode, with the replacement computed there or committed
unverified as EOS. In every case each reply and each sink counter equal
those of the run with no connector. Each case also fails under a
mutation of the code it depends on: the load mapping, the hit cap, the
reset of a failed load, the overrun mark, the end of the replay after a
preemption, the clear of draft zero at a preemption (in the chunked
resume only), both pins of an aborted load (one pin alone keeps the case
passing), a block hashed with a held token that is then rejected, the
hash of a block completed by an unverified token, and a rollback missing
for a rejected draft zero or for every rejection. The other 10 tests
cover the allow rule. No connector, and the connector with the CPU or
the disk backend, pass, while the roles kv_producer and kv_consumer,
NixlConnector, LMCacheConnectorV1, a MultiConnector around the allowed
connector, a kv_connector_module_path, and a config with no
kv_connector are refused by name. The offload suite with the connector
unit test passed 278 tests, the 58 new ones included, with 2 skipped
because they need CUDA, and the sink scheduler suite passed 65.

On three DGX Spark nodes, with spark-ds41f-1120 at tp3-triangle-4k and
the whole series mounted over the release image, each rank started
with rejection on beside the tier. The engine counted 38 draft-zero
rejections during the checks, every compared disk hit returned the
tokens of a hit in the GPU pool, and no rank logged an assertion. No
check forced a rejection right after a tier hit. The check in the V2
runner was not tested on the CPU test setup, where the module does not
import, and neither were the drafter state after a tier hit under a
forced rejection and a preemption on hardware.

Upstream status: the patch is proposed to spark-ds41f in
experiments/2026-11-22-kv-disk-tier and has not been submitted to
local-inference-lab/vllm.
